A||N||13||WhiteFieroBlur||Car just got lowered now everything is messed up||||1||
Z||000000||WhiteFieroBlur||12-21-2001||10:06 PM||||Well I just got my car lowered and didn't get in aliment. I am going to tommorrow. But when I tried to drive it home today it could not keep it on the road. I was fish tailing the whole way home. I can also hear some metal on metal kinda noise on my back tire that wasn't there. when I move my car manually I don't hear it like its a caliper sticking its when I get in it and drive it. also my steering is so loose and the steeringwheel is all crooked but I suppose thats from needing to be alined. I came home and thought he didn't tighten my back tire but its tightenened. Am I suppose to have all these problems after its lowered?<P> I am scared to drive it tomorrow I was driving at like 20 mph cause I was scared I was gonna spin out or something from the car fish tailing. So what could be wrong?||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||12-21-2001||10:25 PM||||If you have your car lowered you change all of the suspension geometry. I would not drive it fast anywhere until you get it aligned. You could try a home alignment to make it a little better, use the search engine to find info on this, but get it aligned fast. Hope this helps. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||8Ball||12-21-2001||10:26 PM||||Well when you disassemble the suspension to lower the car, you tend to severly mess up the alignment. But it sounds like the guy that did it, did not even bother to try to eyeball it back semi-straight.<BR>Get an alignment tomorrow, have it towed if possible. Then all should be ok. Although I am curious about the rubbing sound.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-21-2001||11:46 PM||||Do the wheels look like they are all pointing straight? If its as bad as you say, I'd expect evertyhing to be so fungled up everything would be pointing in the wrong directions. You can just eyeball the alignment to make it somewhat driveable, to a shop anyway.||||reg||11
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||12-21-2001||11:57 PM||||Your wheel alignment is off (caster) and your wheels are probably rubbing against the shock or spring. once you get that fixed it won't make that noise anymore. I just put lowering springs on my GT and had the same problems that you're having. Once you get the car realighned it'll be back to normal.<BR>One word of caution though: with your wheel rubbing like it is it might rip the wheel weights off the wheel if you have them on the inside of the wheel! That would mean that you're going to have to have them rebalanced too. This is only if the wheel weights are on the inside though.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000005||BlackStang95||12-22-2001||12:09 PM||||Damn.  That's why I'll never lower my car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ITB 2M4||12-22-2001||12:40 PM||||Well, the caster is not adjustable on a Fiero, not without a big hammer anyway.  More than likely, your camber and toe are out of wack though.  Especially if the car was in the air when the suspension was put back together.  I would imagine the positive camber along with toe in at the rear is whats making the car feel 'loose' at the back.  If you can, loosen the bolts in the strut towers, and push in on the strut body, while tightening the bolts back up.  This will help you get the negative camber back into the rear suspension.  Depending on where your steering rack now sits in realtion to the front knuckle, you'll have toe in or toe out.  Toe out will make the steering more responsive, taking away from the 'push' that GM built into the Fiero.  It will also make the rear feel a little more lively as well.  Get a set of MOOG Problem Solver ball joints so you can get some camber adjustment in the front end as well.  There are a few different versions of this ball joint, all with the same part number, for the Fiero.  Get the ones with the castle nut and cotter pin.  These seem to have the most camber adjustment.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000007||watts||12-22-2001||12:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ITB 2M4:<BR><B>Well, the caster is not adjustable on a Fiero</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Beg to differ - on the front it is slightly adjustable.  There are two shims on the upper control arm, one 9mm, one 3mm.  By switching these around into various configurations, you can vary the caster.<P>But everything else is right.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||12-22-2001||01:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Damn.  That's why I'll never lower my car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Correction... That's why I'll never do a shitty lowering  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>If I pay someone to lower my car, be better align it, lest he find an old strut-tower inserted in him... CROSSWAYS.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000009||WhiteFieroBlur||12-22-2001||07:01 PM||||Well I tottaly picked the wrong time to lower it. Well I still hadn't had a chance to align it and tomorrow is sunday and I doubt anyone is open. So am I not suppose to drive it at all till monday to get it aligned? I have only driven it a few miles back and forth to work and home. I feel like my back tires are sliding side ways on the road. So am I hurting it by driving? I asked the guy at the shop and he said that you can wait a month but get it aligned quick and I feel like its tottaly undrivable cause I can't stay straight on the road I look like a drunk driving my car at 20 mph lol.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||12-23-2001||01:22 AM||||Besides making the car unstable, alignment problems (especially as bad as you say) will eat your tires really quick. I wouldn't drive it, except in extreme emergency... Better safe than sorry, if it's that unstable.||||reg||6
Z||000011||Oreif||12-23-2001||01:44 AM||||ANY car lowered requires an alignment. You are changing the geometry of the suspension.<BR>Do not drive it when the alignment is off far enough to where 20mph is unstable. This will wear tires and put undo pressure on other parts like ball joints and tierod ends. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||12-23-2001||04:44 AM||||Most alignment shops arent willing to take off the upper a arm to move the shims. Theyd rather just say its 'in specs' and give it back to you. Time costs them money. With Christmas rite around the corner, Id say your going to be lucky if you can get it done before the end of the week. Sounds like another case of 'if it aint broke, dont fix it'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave Gunsul||12-23-2001||05:21 AM||||Ooops, I meant to say camber not caster.<BR>If you adjust that it will at least keep the wheels from rubbing as I stated above but you still need to adjust the toe before driving it or you'll end up in a ditch, or worse yet hitting someone else.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||11
